<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The liturgy is not a show</h4>
<p>Many people (especially young people) stop going to Mass because they <em>&laquo;don't like it&raquo;</em> or <em>&laquo;get bored.&raquo;</em> Help them understand that the liturgy does not depend on our taste. Christ is present and acts regardless of whether the choir sings well or the homily is exciting. The question is not <em>&laquo;Did I like it?&raquo;</em> but <em>&laquo;Did I encounter Christ?&raquo;</em></p>

<h4>Common priesthood</h4>
<p>It is important that the faithful know they are not spectators. Every baptized person has an active role in the liturgy: responding, singing, offering. Ask: <em>&laquo;If all the laity were spectators, why does the priest say 'The Lord be with you' and wait for a response?&raquo;</em></p>

<h4>The Paschal Mystery</h4>
<p>Explain that the Mass is not a repetition of Christ's sacrifice, but its making-present. Christ offered himself once for all (Heb 7:27), and at Mass that one sacrifice is made present. It is not a new sacrifice, but the same one.</p>

<h4>Signs and symbols</h4>
<p>Young people especially need to understand the signs: why water, why bread, why incense. They are not empty rituals; they are a language that speaks to body and soul. Help them <em>&laquo;read&raquo;</em> the signs.</p>

<h4>Sunday</h4>
<p>Remember that Sunday is not <em>&laquo;the day of obligation&raquo;</em> as a burden, but the Lord's Day, the <em>&laquo;little Easter.&raquo;</em> It is a gift, not a burden. The Church asks us to attend Sunday Mass because we need it like the bread we break.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of a family dinner: Mass is God's dinner with his children. Activities: draw the objects used at Mass (chalice, paten, candles), color a simple liturgical calendar. Teach the responses of the Mass.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience: boredom at Mass, difficulty concentrating. Ask: <em>&laquo;What movie or concert keeps your attention? What makes an experience captivating? What if Mass is more real than any concert?&raquo;</em></p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults come from traditions where liturgy was less structured. Help them understand the value of the liturgy: it is not rigidity, but communion with the universal Church. Explain the key moments of the Mass.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of active participation (<em>participatio actuosa</em>) from Vatican II. Relate the liturgy to the Trinity: the Father is glorified, the Son acts, the Holy Spirit unifies. You can quote Romano Guardini, <em>&laquo;The Spirit of the Liturgy.&raquo;</em></p>

</section>

